package pages

import "github.com/atknatk/tappa/web/templates/components"

// The ANOMALIES section (M6-11) — one screen that says "there is something odd here".
//
// 🔴 THE SENTENCE THE WHOLE SCREEN IS BUILT AROUND: the report does not comment, it
// shows data. There is no "suspicious employee" label anywhere below, no severity, no
// score and no ranking of people. Every listing prints a count beside the denominator
// it came out of, and every threshold the page filtered by is printed next to the list
// it filtered — so a reader can disagree with the screen using the screen's own numbers.
//
// 🔴 IT RENDERS PanelShell, NOT PanelShellWithScript. Nothing here needs a script:
// changing week is a link. That keeps the panel's widened Content-Security-Policy on
// exactly one url, which is the cardinality
// TestPanelScreens_ScriptsAndPolicyAgreeAndReachNoThirdParty pins.
//
// 🔴 §4.3 — THIS IS A READ. No form posts, no button dismisses a signal, and nothing
// marks a row as seen. A dismissed anomaly is one the next reader never sees, and
// `transactions` takes no writes at all.
//
// 🔴 §4.6 — WHAT THIS SCREEN CANNOT ANSWER FOR IS PRINTED ON IT. Three of the signals
// are read out of a frozen policy snapshot that older records do not carry; the count
// of those records is on the page, in words, rather than being quietly folded into
// "clean". Two more signals the M6-11 card asks for have NO source in this product at
// all, and the page says which two and why rather than leaving a reader to assume they
// were checked.
//
// skill tappa-brand governs the surface: kitchen docket as the record motif, every
// number in IBM Plex Mono, the fixed status-to-colour mapping (a thing to look at is
// saffron, a refusal is tomato, a neutral count is the quiet `line` tone), and no
// meaning carried by colour alone — every chip says its own word.
templ AdminAnomalies(v AnomaliesView) {
	@PanelShell(v.PanelChrome) {
		@anomalyWeekBar(v)
		<p class="font-mono text-sm text-ink/85">
			{ v.WeekLabel }
			<span class="text-ink/70">· { v.ZoneLabel }</span>
		</p>
		if v.Queried {
			@anomalyTotals(v)
			@anomalyMissingSources()
			@anomalyPeople(v)
			@anomalyVenues(v)
			@anomalyPlaques(v)
			@anomalyPairs(v)
			@anomalyOpen(v)
			@anomalyPolicies(v)
		}
	}
}

// anomalyWeekBar is the week picker. A plain GET form with no script, so every week is
// an address — bookmarkable, shareable, and working on a locked-down laptop.
templ anomalyWeekBar(v AnomaliesView) {
	<form class="filter-bar" method="get" action={ templ.URL(v.ThisWeekHref) } aria-label="Choose a week">
		<div class="filter-field">
			<label class="docket-label" for="anomaly-week">Week containing</label>
			<input class="filter-input" id="anomaly-week" type="date" name="week" value={ v.WeekValue }/>
		</div>
		<div class="filter-actions">
			<button class="btn btn--primary" type="submit">Show</button>
			<a class="btn btn--quiet" href={ templ.URL(v.PrevHref) }>Previous week</a>
			<a class="btn btn--quiet" href={ templ.URL(v.NextHref) }>Next week</a>
			<a class="btn btn--quiet" href={ templ.URL(v.ThisWeekHref) }>This week</a>
		</div>
	</form>
}

// anomalyTotals is the week in one docket.
//
// 🔴 EVERY LINE CARRIES ITS OWN DENOMINATOR, which is the difference between a report
// and an accusation. "47 taps" says nothing; "47 of 2 273 records judged on evidence"
// is something a manager can weigh against last week.
templ anomalyTotals(v AnomaliesView) {
	<section class="docket">
		<p class="docket-label">Records examined this week</p>
		<p class="font-mono text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink">{ v.Totals.RecordsLine }</p>
		<p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink/85">
			{ v.Totals.JudgedLine }
			Training taps and manager-typed rows are counted here — a training tap is a real
			touch of a plaque, and a typed row carries no touch at all. Both are named below.
		</p>
		// 🔴 THE TWO DENOMINATORS, SPELLED OUT. The figures below do not all divide by
		// the same population, and a reader who notices two different bases without
		// being told why concludes one of them is wrong. Both numbers are printed so
		// each figure's base can be matched to the population it names.
		<p class="mt-1 text-xs text-ink/70">{ v.Totals.BasisLine }</p>
		<div class="mt-3 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
			if v.Totals.DistanceOnly > 0 {
				<span class="tally tally--queued">{ v.Totals.DistanceOnlyLine }</span>
			}
			if v.Totals.OutsideVenue > 0 {
				<span class="tally tally--queued">{ v.Totals.OutsideVenueLine }</span>
			}
			if v.Totals.CounterGaps > 0 {
				<span class="tally tally--rejected">{ v.Totals.CounterGapsLine }</span>
			}
			if v.Totals.OtherVenue > 0 {
				<span class="tally tally--manual">{ v.Totals.OtherVenueLine }</span>
			}
			if v.Totals.Training > 0 {
				<span class="tally tally--manual">{ v.Totals.TrainingLine }</span>
			}
			if v.Totals.ManagerTyped > 0 {
				<span class="tally tally--manual">{ v.Totals.ManagerTypedLine }</span>
			}
		</div>
		if v.Totals.OutsideVenue > 0 {
			<p class="mt-3 text-xs text-ink/85">
				{ v.Totals.OutsideVenueOnNetworkLine }
			</p>
		}
		if v.Totals.Unanswerable > 0 {
			// 🔴 §4.6 — "WE CANNOT ANSWER FOR THESE" IS NOT "THESE ARE CLEAN". Counter
			// gaps, the outside-the-venue reading and the other-venue count are all read
			// out of the frozen policy snapshot a record carries. Records written before
			// that column existed, and every manager-typed row, do not have one.
			<p class="mt-2 text-xs text-ink/70">{ v.Totals.UnanswerableLine }</p>
		}
	</section>
}

// anomalyMissingSources is the paragraph this section owes its reader most.
//
// 🔴 TWO OF THE SIGNALS THE M6-11 CARD ASKS FOR HAVE NO SOURCE IN THIS PRODUCT, and a
// screen that simply omitted them would read as "checked, nothing found". Both are
// named, with what would have to be built to answer them. The words are the honest
// ones: this page cannot see these things, rather than these things did not happen.
//
// 🔴 THE THIRD ITEM IS A LIMIT OF THE PAIR LISTING'S OWN QUERY, and it was written in
// the SQL and nowhere a reader could see it. A limit only the author knows about is a
// limit the reader is not told about, which is the same failure as an omitted signal.
// The heading counts no longer: it said "Two things" while the block is a list, and a
// count in a heading is one more thing to keep in step with the list under it.
templ anomalyMissingSources() {
	@components.Notice(components.ToneWarn, "What this page does not see") {
		<p>
			A plaque page that was opened and never used is not recorded anywhere, so the
			number of opened-but-unused pages is not on this screen. What IS on it is the
			counter gap above: the plaque's own chip counts every read, so collected pages
			leave a jump behind whether or not they are ever used.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-2">
			Which device a person signed in from is not recorded either — only a coarse
			browser family, which every phone of the same make shares. So "several people
			activated from one device" cannot be told from "several people use the same
			model of phone", and this page does not guess at it.
		</p>
		<p class="mt-2">
			The list of people who tap together compares each tap with the ONE before it at
			that venue. Where three people arrive in a burst, the first and the third are
			not paired with each other — the two neighbouring pairs are listed instead, so
			the group is visible but that one combination is not.
		</p>
	}
}

// anomalyPeople lists the employees carrying at least one signal.
//
// 🔴 THE PEOPLE WITH NOTHING ON THEM ARE NOT LISTED, AND THAT IS A FILTER ON THE DATA
// RATHER THAN A VERDICT ON ANYBODY. A list of the whole roster with zeroes beside it is
// the employees section with extra columns; what is left out here is rows, not facts —
// the totals above cover everybody.
templ anomalyPeople(v AnomaliesView) {
	<h2 class="font-display text-lg font-bold tracking-tight text-ink">Per person</h2>
	if len(v.People) > 0 {
		<p class="text-xs text-ink/70">
			Only people with at least one of these counts above zero appear here. The
			totals above cover everybody who tapped.
		</p>
		if v.MorePeople {
			@components.Notice(components.ToneWarn, "This list is shortened") {
				<p>{ moreRowsLine("people", v.ListCap) }</p>
			}
		}
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
			for _, p := range v.People {
				<section class="docket">
					<div class="report-head">
						<p class="report-who">{ p.Name }</p>
						<p class="report-total">{ p.Records }</p>
					</div>
					<div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
						if p.DistanceOnly != "" {
							<span class="tally tally--queued">{ p.DistanceOnly }</span>
						}
						if p.OutsideVenue != "" {
							<span class="tally tally--queued">{ p.OutsideVenue }</span>
						}
						if p.CounterGaps != "" {
							<span class="tally tally--rejected">{ p.CounterGaps }</span>
						}
						if p.OtherVenue != "" {
							<span class="tally tally--manual">{ p.OtherVenue }</span>
						}
					</div>
				</section>
			}
		</div>
	} else {
		@components.EmptyState("Nobody carries one of these counts this week") {
			<p>
				Every person who tapped is inside the totals above; none of them has a
				GPS-only tap, a counter gap, a reading outside the venue or a tap at
				another venue in this week.
			</p>
		}
	}
}

// anomalyVenues is the same week split by the venue whose plaque was touched.
//
// EVERY VENUE WITH TAPS IS HERE, INCLUDING THE CLEAN ONES, and that is deliberate: a
// high rate at one branch beside a zero at another is the comparison this section
// exists to make, and it needs the zero to be visible.
templ anomalyVenues(v AnomaliesView) {
	if len(v.Venues) > 0 {
		<h2 class="font-display text-lg font-bold tracking-tight text-ink">Per venue</h2>
		<p class="text-xs text-ink/70">
			Every venue with taps this week, clean ones included. A high rate at one venue
			beside a low one at another is as likely to be a Wi-Fi problem as anything else
			— both are worth knowing.
		</p>
		if v.MoreVenues {
			@components.Notice(components.ToneWarn, "This list is shortened") {
				<p>{ moreRowsLine("venues", v.ListCap) }</p>
			}
		}
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
			for _, l := range v.Venues {
				<section class="docket">
					<div class="report-head">
						<p class="report-who">{ l.Name }</p>
						<p class="report-total">{ l.Records }</p>
					</div>
					<div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
						<span class="tally tally--queued">{ l.DistanceOnly }</span>
						<span class="tally tally--queued">{ l.OutsideVenue }</span>
					</div>
				</section>
			}
		</div>
	}
}

// anomalyPlaques lists the wall tags whose counter jumped.
//
// 🔴 THE COUNTER GAP IS THE ONE LIVE TRACE OF COLLECTED TAP URLs (A1 / Q21). The chip
// increments on every read whether or not the page is ever used, so a run of unused
// reads shows up as a jump the next time somebody really taps.
templ anomalyPlaques(v AnomaliesView) {
	if len(v.Plaques) > 0 {
		<h2 class="font-display text-lg font-bold tracking-tight text-ink">Plaque counter gaps</h2>
		<p class="text-sm text-ink/85">
			A plaque's chip counts every read. A gap means reads happened that never became
			a check-in — a page opened and abandoned looks the same as a page collected for
			later, and this list does not tell them apart.
		</p>
		if v.MorePlaques {
			@components.Notice(components.ToneWarn, "This list is shortened") {
				<p>{ moreRowsLine("plaques", v.ListCap) }</p>
			}
		}
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
			for _, p := range v.Plaques {
				<section class="docket">
					<div class="report-head">
						<p class="report-who">{ p.LocationName }</p>
						<p class="report-total">{ p.LargestGap }</p>
					</div>
					<p class="docket-label mt-1">Plaque { p.TagUID }</p>
					<div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
						<span class="tally tally--rejected">{ p.CounterGaps }</span>
						<span class="tally tally--manual">{ p.Records }</span>
					</div>
				</section>
			}
		</div>
	}
}

// anomalyPairs lists people whose taps repeatedly land together.
//
// 🔴 §5 SAYS A QUEUE IS NORMAL — different people touching one plaque one after another
// is the ordinary start of a shift. So a single burst is not on this list at all; what
// is on it is a pair that does it on separate days, and the thresholds are printed so a
// reader can judge the filter rather than trust it.
templ anomalyPairs(v AnomaliesView) {
	if len(v.Pairs) > 0 {
		<h2 class="font-display text-lg font-bold tracking-tight text-ink">Tapped together, more than once</h2>
		<p class="text-sm text-ink/85">
			Two people arriving together is not a finding. This is a place to look, not a
			conclusion: colleagues who share a lift produce exactly this row.
		</p>
		<p class="font-mono text-xs text-ink/85">{ v.TogetherLine }</p>
		if v.MorePairs {
			@components.Notice(components.ToneWarn, "This list is shortened") {
				<p>{ moreRowsLine("pairs", v.ListCap) }</p>
			}
		}
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
			for _, p := range v.Pairs {
				<section class="docket">
					<div class="report-head">
						<p class="report-who">{ p.First } &amp; { p.Second }</p>
						<p class="report-total">{ p.Days }</p>
					</div>
					<p class="docket-label mt-1">{ p.Venue }</p>
					<div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
						<span class="tally tally--queued">{ p.Together }</span>
						<span class="tally tally--queued">{ p.Closest }</span>
					</div>
				</section>
			}
		</div>
	}
}

// anomalyOpen is the check-ins nothing closed, plus the tally the list itself cannot
// produce.
templ anomalyOpen(v AnomaliesView) {
	<h2 class="font-display text-lg font-bold tracking-tight text-ink">Check-ins with no checkout</h2>
	// 🔴 THE DEFINITION IS ON THE PAGE BECAUSE IT IS NOT THE REPORTS SECTION'S. Here a
	// check-in is open when no checkout follows it, full stop. The reports section
	// PAIRS a week's records and additionally treats a very late checkout as a missed
	// one, so the two counts can legitimately differ — and a reader who finds two
	// numbers with one name is a reader who stops trusting both.
	<p class="text-sm text-ink/85">
		A check-in with no later checkout, anywhere in the record. The Reports section
		counts a slightly different thing — it pairs a week's records into shifts — so
		the two figures can differ without either being wrong.
	</p>
	<p class="text-xs text-ink/70">
		Training taps are not in this list. A training tap is a check-in nothing ever
		closes, so counting it would add one false entry for every new starter.
	</p>
	if len(v.Open) > 0 {
		if v.MoreOpen {
			@components.Notice(components.ToneWarn, "This list is shortened") {
				<p>{ moreRowsLine("check-ins", v.ListCap) }</p>
			}
		}
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
			for _, o := range v.Open {
				<section class="docket">
					<div class="report-head">
						<p class="report-who">{ o.Who }</p>
						<p class="report-day">{ o.At }</p>
					</div>
					<p class="docket-label mt-1">{ o.Venue }</p>
					<div class="mt-2 flex flex-wrap gap-2">
						<span class="tally tally--queued">Still open</span>
						if o.ManagerTyped {
							<span class="tally tally--manual">Entered by a manager</span>
						}
						if o.WasMaskable {
							<span class="tally tally--manual">Training tap follows</span>
						}
					</div>
				</section>
			}
		</div>
	} else {
		@components.EmptyState("Every check-in this week has a checkout after it") {
			<p>Nothing in this week is waiting for a missing record.</p>
		}
	}
	if v.Masked.Total > 0 {
		// 🔴 THE HALF THE LIST ABOVE CANNOT SHOW (ADR 0008, M5-11's hand-over note). A
		// single checkout satisfies "nothing closed it" for EVERY older unclosed
		// check-in at once, so an entry a training tap once hid leaves the list silently
		// on the person's next checkout — counted as closed, by a checkout that really
		// belonged to a different check-in. Finding those needs this second question.
		@components.Notice(components.ToneWarn, "Check-ins with a training tap after them") {
			<p>
				A training tap sitting after a check-in used to hide it, so the next tap was
				recorded as another arrival instead of a departure. Those records are still
				here — nothing was lost — but a later checkout takes them off the list above
				without closing what they opened.
			</p>
			<p class="mt-2 font-mono text-xs">
				{ v.Masked.StillOpen } · { v.Masked.ClosedLater }
			</p>
			<p class="mt-2 text-xs">
				This is a hint about where to look rather than a diagnosis: a new starter who
				taps again after training produces the same shape.
			</p>
		}
	}
}

// anomalyPolicies is the rule breakdown (M3-07).
//
// 🔴 IT IS THE ONE BLOCK HERE THAT IS NOT A SUSPICION. A guardrail firing is the system
// working. What is worth seeing is a CHANGE in the mix — a week where the invalid-tap
// rule triples is a week where a plaque is failing or somebody is replaying URLs, and
// neither shows up in a verdict count.
templ anomalyPolicies(v AnomaliesView) {
	if len(v.Policies) > 0 {
		<h2 class="font-display text-lg font-bold tracking-tight text-ink">Which rule decided</h2>
		<p class="text-sm text-ink/85">
			Every record names the rule that judged it. A rule firing is not a problem in
			itself; a rule firing far more than last week is worth a look.
		</p>
		if v.MorePolicies {
			@components.Notice(components.ToneWarn, "This list is shortened") {
				<p>{ moreRowsLine("rules", v.ListCap) }</p>
			}
		}
		<div class="flex flex-col gap-4">
			for _, s := range v.Policies {
				<section class="docket">
					<div class="report-head">
						<p class="report-who">{ s.SID }</p>
						<p class="report-total">{ s.Records }</p>
					</div>
					if s.Layer != "" {
						<p class="docket-label mt-1">{ s.Layer }</p>
					}
				</section>
			}
		</div>
	}
}

// moreRowsLine is the one sentence every shortened listing prints. It names the cap
// once rather than repeating the number in six places, and it says what is NOT
// shortened — because the totals at the top of the page cover everything.
func moreRowsLine(kind string, cap int) string {
	return "More " + kind + " match than one page lists, so this list stops at " +
		itoa(cap) + ". The counts at the top of the page cover every record."
}
